<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Results of CFR</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed Color Fidelity Refinement (CFR) pipeline for color fidelity enhancement can be seamlessly integrated into any diffusion-based generative model employing the standard classifier-free guidance mechanism.
As shown in Tab. <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.4 Results of CFR ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ Too Vivid to Be Real? Benchmarking and Calibrating Generative Color Fidelity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we apply the CFM-based CFR pipeline to SD3.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, PixArt-<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and Hunyuan-DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and report the quantitative results before and after enhancement in terms of FID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, CLIPScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, saturation difference (<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>Sat.) and CFM.
Compared with the original outputs, models equipped with the CFM-driven CFR produce images with more natural and visually realistic color appearances.
As shown in Tab. <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.4 Results of CFR ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ Too Vivid to Be Real? Benchmarking and Calibrating Generative Color Fidelity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, saturation values decrease by 0.08–0.11 and CFM scores increase by 1.3–2.0 points across all three models, while maintaining comparable image quality (FID) and semantic alignment (CLIPScore).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10990v1/agr_vis.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/344;" width="685" height="344" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative examples of different CFR variants.<span id="S6.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> ‘Origin’ denotes the original image before applying CFR, while ‘CFR’ represents the results enhanced by the corresponding model-based CFR. The bottom-right value in each image indicates its CFM score, where a higher score reflects better color fidelity.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
